<%# The "you at a glance" header — avatar, name, address — at the top of BOTH
    /profile and /profile/edit.

    THE AVATAR BADGE IS THE ONLY WAY THROUGH TO EDITING from the read page — the
    operator removed the "Profile" heading and the top-right Edit button so the
    card is the page. That makes the badge load-bearing rather than decorative,
    which is why it is a real <a>/<button> with an aria-label rather than a
    styled div: losing it to a keyboard or a screen reader now means losing the
    edit page entirely.

    Locals:
      user      (required)
      editable  (Boolean) — true on the edit page, where the avatar button opens
                the crop modal. On the read page it LINKS to /profile/edit
                instead, so the same affordance in the same place does the
                obvious thing on each page.

    IT IS A HEADER, NOT A ROW. It was a section in the registry until the operator
    drew it as the identity block iOS Settings puts at the top of the page: a
    thing you look at, with one control on it. Rows are things you change.
%>
<% editable = local_assigns.fetch(:editable, false) %>
<%# THE AVATAR IS OPTIONAL, and this guard is the one the registry used to carry.
    As a ROW the avatar declared `requires: :avatar` and was dropped whole on a
    host whose model has no attachment. Moving it into this header lost that
    protection — the first render against such a model raised
    `undefined method 'avatar'`, caught by the thin-host suite. A header is not
    exempt from the rule that consumers disagree about their users table. %>
<% attachable = user.respond_to?(:avatar) && user.avatar.respond_to?(:attached?) %>

<div class="card p-6 mb-6 flex flex-col items-center text-center">
  <%# The avatar and its edit affordance. The badge sits on the circle's
      lower-right — the position the operator's illustration used for a marker,
      now carrying an action instead. %>
  <div class="relative mb-3" style="width: 128px; height: 128px;">
    <% if attachable && user.avatar.attached? %>
      <%= image_tag user.avatar, class: "rounded-full object-cover",
                    style: "width: 128px; height: 128px;", alt: user.display_name %>
    <% else %>
      <div class="rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-white text-4xl"
           style="width: 128px; height: 128px; background-color: <%= user.avatar_color %>">
        <%= user.avatar_initials %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# A BUTTON on the edit page, a LINK on the read page — same glyph, same
        spot, and each does what that page can do. Both are real controls rather
        than a div, so a keyboard reaches them. %>
    <% badge_class = "absolute bottom-0 right-0 flex items-center justify-center rounded-full border-2 border-page bg-primary text-white hover:opacity-90 transition" %>
    <% badge_style = "width: 36px; height: 36px;" %>
    <% if editable && attachable %>
      <button type="button" @click="$refs.filePicker.click()"
              aria-label="Change your profile photo"
              class="<%= badge_class %>" style="<%= badge_style %>">
        <%= render "studio/profiles/pencil_icon" %>
      </button>
    <% elsif !editable %>
      <%= link_to edit_profile_path, aria: { label: "Edit your profile" },
                  class: badge_class, style: badge_style do %>
        <%= render "studio/profiles/pencil_icon" %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <p class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-heading"><%= user.display_name %></p>
  <% if user.respond_to?(:email) && user.email.present? %>
    <p class="text-secondary text-sm break-all"><%= user.email %></p>
  <% end %>
</div>
